WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.261 --> 00:00:01.744
- Oh, my goodness.
- Hello.

00:00:01.744 --> 00:00:04.256
- What a beautiful studio.

00:00:04.256 --> 00:00:05.580
- I know. We're excited, thank you.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:08.330
- They told me they didn't
have money for nice studios.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.100
- I love being in this building with you.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:11.940
- It's a great building.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:13.770
- I have to say this, you
are the very first person,

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:15.600
when we announced that we
were coming to New York,

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.390
he sent me a handwritten card.

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:19.830
Saying, "Welcome to 30 Rock.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:21.180
I'll show you around the commissary."

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:22.560
Which by the way, has really good food.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.210
- It's an excellent commissary.

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:25.043
That was the first thing
I was gonna tell you.

00:00:25.043 --> 00:00:26.700
- When you said it in the card,

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:28.583
and you brought up food,
I was like, I don't know.

00:00:28.583 --> 00:00:32.160
Because a cafeteria, it can be suspect

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:33.660
- I started here in 2001,

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:36.270
and the cafeteria in 2001 was a dump.

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:37.770
- Okay, okay. I came at the right time.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:41.400
- They rebuilt the commissary
and I take it to the streets.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:42.780
I brag about it everywhere I go.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:44.700
- It's so good.
- Yeah, it's really great.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:46.200
You do have to pay for the food.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.303
I've heard about you at previous places.

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:51.150
There's an assumption because
you're Kelly Clarkson,

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:52.800
you can just take the
food and walk right out.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.939
But I do need you to stop.
- Well, I'm here now 30 Rock.

00:00:54.939 --> 00:00:56.880
I want free food. (laughs)

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:58.290
No, I love the building.

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:01.440
But I know that you have a
crazy story with your doorman.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:03.540
- Yeah, first of all,
it's so exciting to be

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:04.380
in a room with all these

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:05.523
New York City doorman.
- I know.

00:01:05.523 --> 00:01:08.228
(audience applauds)

00:01:08.228 --> 00:01:09.870
- [Kelly] Because you see them every day.

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:12.090
- But I'll say, because you're all here,

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:13.200
I walked to the building,

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.223
there are so many unsigned
for packages outside.

00:01:16.223 --> 00:01:17.850
(Kelly and audience laugh)

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:18.683
Buildings right now.
- It's ridiculous.

00:01:18.683 --> 00:01:20.938
- Just littering the streets.

00:01:20.938 --> 00:01:22.463
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:22.463 --> 00:01:24.960
Yeah, I think I have
the best doorman story.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:25.793
- You do.
- In New York City.

00:01:25.793 --> 00:01:28.320
Which is my wife, we were
on our way to deliver...

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.020
She was gonna deliver, we decided.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:33.360
She was pregnant with our second.

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:36.240
Her water broke and we
went out to the Uber.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:37.620
And we walked right back inside

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:39.360
because she said, "It's
happening right now."

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:41.370
And so she gave birth in our very...

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:42.690
We have a very small lobby.

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:44.310
When I say lobby, I think people

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:45.540
picture something a lot bigger.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:46.800
It is not grand.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.160
And my wife fully gave birth in our lobby

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.590
and we had two doormen on that day.

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:54.930
And I would say one of
them rose to the occasion

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:57.224
and the other one sort
of sunk to the floor.

00:01:57.224 --> 00:01:59.550
- The other one's like
the Homer Simpson gif.

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:00.480
You know, like backing into the bush.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:03.813
- He did very quietly sort of
became part of the wallpaper.

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:05.580
But it's great.

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:07.320
He's now sort of just...

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:10.553
Our our middle son Axle, he's
just part of the building now.

00:02:10.553 --> 00:02:12.480
Everybody loves lobby baby.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:13.620
- I think that's what you don't...

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:15.690
Lobby baby, I love that it's lobby baby.

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:16.523
- [Seth] We call him lobby baby.

00:02:16.523 --> 00:02:18.330
- Do your kids know this
now that they're older?

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:19.163
- Do they know?
- Yes.

00:02:19.163 --> 00:02:20.822
So we got three.

00:02:20.822 --> 00:02:22.230
(audience applauds)

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:23.250
By the way-
- Oh my god, so cute.

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:24.960
- In case you're
wondering, which is the kid

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:25.860
that was born in the lobby,

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:27.390
it's the one with a box on his head.

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:31.110
So it it is having long lasting effects.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:34.320
Yeah, they know he's
lobby and it's really...

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.420
I feel for my seven-year-old,
because they'll go out

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:37.980
and I'll be in the park
and people will come up

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:39.750
and they'll say, "Which
one is lobby baby?"

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:42.000
And the older one says, "He's lobby baby.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.489
I'm hospital baby."

00:02:44.489 --> 00:02:45.322
(audience laughs)
- Oh.

00:02:45.322 --> 00:02:48.120
- And I don't have the heart
to tell him nobody cares.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:51.803
Hospital baby is the most
common of all the babies.

00:02:51.803 --> 00:02:52.921
- Oh my god, don't tell that.

00:02:52.921 --> 00:02:54.900
- But then we have our
third one, a bathtub baby.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:55.733
Did you know that?

00:02:55.733 --> 00:02:57.390
- No.
- Yeah, not by accident.

00:02:57.390 --> 00:02:58.980
It wasn't like my wife was in a bathroom

00:02:58.980 --> 00:02:59.813
and was like, "Oh, boy."
- You are just trying

00:02:59.813 --> 00:03:00.840
to make all your kids feel special.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:01.830
- Yes.

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:02.760
Yeah, we had a home birth.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:03.660
We had a planned home birth.

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:04.720
- Oh, you planned it?

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:06.240
- Well, the doormen planned it.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.400
They said, we don't...
(audience laughs)

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.080
They said, "We have come
up with a plan for you.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:10.913
When you have the baby,

00:03:10.913 --> 00:03:12.930
Don't come down here again"
- Oh, my god.

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:14.765
Your wife is like a Viking,

00:03:14.765 --> 00:03:15.930
having babies everywhere.

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:16.763
I'm like, "What?"

00:03:16.763 --> 00:03:18.240
- Yeah, she's basically
going through the city.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:20.280
She wants to do Staten Island Ferry next.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:22.410
We're really excited.
- Okay. Okay.

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:23.520
A lot of kids in that house.

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:24.960
- We're gonna have a lot of kids, yeah.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:26.070
- No, you-time anymore.

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:27.780
I'm excited to experience Halloween here

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:29.010
'cause I have kids as well.

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:30.150
So what is trick-or-treating like?

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:31.500
Do your kids have costumes already?

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:33.630
- Yeah, so the five-year-old
always picks right away.

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:36.000
He usually picks like November
3rd of the previous year.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.537
And he's gonna be a gecko from this-

00:03:38.537 --> 00:03:40.050
- Oh, "PJ Masks."
- "PJ Masks."

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:41.100
So he's a gecko.

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:43.200
The older one, he can't make a decision.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.480
He's currently a wasp, but it's too early.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:46.978
That'll change.

00:03:46.978 --> 00:03:48.224
- Like, "The Wasp," the superhero

00:03:48.224 --> 00:03:49.080
or just the wasp?
- Just like a wasp.

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:51.152
- A wasp, got it.

00:03:51.152 --> 00:03:54.330
- He's like, "I just
wanna be a mediocre wasp.

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:55.347
- Just an average wasp.

00:03:55.347 --> 00:03:58.140
- And then, the baby,
we're gonna put the baby

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:00.757
in the same lamb costume
that the kids have worn.

00:04:00.757 --> 00:04:02.070
- Oh, you have one that's passed down?

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:03.220
- We just have... Yeah.

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:05.640
There you go. Those are the lambs.

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:07.110
Those are when the boys were younger.

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:09.180
- That's cute.
- Yeah.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:10.583
- When it was sweet.

00:04:10.583 --> 00:04:11.700
You were like, "When they were younger."

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:14.760
- I was, I'm like, "Oh, I miss those two."

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:16.017
- [Kelly] Remember when they liked me?

00:04:16.017 --> 00:04:19.500
- Now they're walking around
me like, "I wanna be a wasp!"

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.480
- So wait, this is your 10th anniversary?

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:23.280
- We had our 10th
anniversary on September 1st.

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:24.113
Yeah.
- Wow.

00:04:24.113 --> 00:04:24.958
- Pretty exciting.

00:04:24.958 --> 00:04:27.420
(audience applauds)

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:29.280
- Statistics are against, just saying.

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:30.720
- Statistics are against you.

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:33.330
They say for families who
have a child in the lobby,

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:34.694
they almost never make it.

00:04:34.694 --> 00:04:35.970
(Kelly laughs)

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:36.990
- Or maybe that's the key.

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:38.550
You could do anything together, anywhere.

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:40.137
- Have a baby in a lobby,
if you can do that-

00:04:40.137 --> 00:04:40.970
you can save-
- I love that.

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:41.803
- Do you have a secret?

00:04:41.803 --> 00:04:44.220
- We do not have a secret.
- You're just a good listener?

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:46.350
- No, I think we just,
we fit well together.

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:48.840
We have a very complimentary skills.

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:50.663
- That must have been my problem. Okay.

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:54.630
Your podcast, you have it
with your brother, right?

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:55.627
- Yeah, we have a podcast called

00:04:55.627 --> 00:04:57.270
"Family Trips with the Myers Brothers."

00:04:57.270 --> 00:04:58.500
And it's me and my brother.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:00.690
We talk to it's celebrities
and interesting people

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:03.420
about the trips they took
as kids and almost no...

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:04.470
There we are as kids, look at us.

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:05.790
- [Kelly] Like when we
never wore seat belts?

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:06.623
- Yes.
- Yeah.

00:05:06.623 --> 00:05:07.803
- Nobody.
- Nope.

00:05:07.803 --> 00:05:10.823
- It's amazing how many of
us lived to tell the tale.

00:05:10.823 --> 00:05:11.656
- [Kelly] I know.

00:05:11.656 --> 00:05:13.470
- I mean, in the 70s it
was just so many kids

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:15.600
just sliding around in the
back of station wagons.

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:17.040
- Or they'd put one of the kids up

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:19.456
in the window shield part in the back.

00:05:19.456 --> 00:05:20.289
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:20.289 --> 00:05:21.300
- And it was a lot of, you talk to people

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:22.620
and they're like, "Yeah,
our parents would put

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:24.990
a card table in the back
of the station wagon.

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:26.760
We'd play poker for eight hours."

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:27.660
You can't believe how little...

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:29.430
- I rode in the back of the
truck, the pickup truck.

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:31.117
Now it's illegal, but I was like,

00:05:31.117 --> 00:05:32.700
"This is the greatest thing ever."

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:34.140
- Very happy to say, everybody survived

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:35.147
and they have good
stories about being kids.

00:05:35.147 --> 00:05:36.720
- I love the podcast with
your brother, though.

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:37.560
That's so cool.

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:38.730
I love my sister so much.

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:39.870
That would be so much fun to do with her.

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:40.950
- It's nice. You need a reason

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:43.170
to talk to your siblings one hour a week.

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:44.160
And that's what we found.

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:46.268
- Well, my sister and I
talk to each other a lot.

00:05:46.268 --> 00:05:47.101
(Seth laughs)

00:05:47.101 --> 00:05:49.710
But any memorable trips with your kids?

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:50.543
- The most memorable for me-

00:05:50.543 --> 00:05:51.810
- Do they wear seat belts now?

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:53.790
- They do, double seat belts.

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:55.320
We took our kids to Uruguay.

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:57.390
My wife has very grand ideas of what

00:05:57.390 --> 00:05:59.247
we can accomplish with young kids.

00:05:59.247 --> 00:06:01.560
And Uruguay, one of the most
beautiful place I've ever been.

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:02.760
But our flight got delayed

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:05.430
and so it was supposed to
be 12 hours, it was 24.

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:07.680
And I was the most tired I've ever been.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:09.870
And at one point, Axel was two,

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:11.130
and I had his stroller

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:13.410
and I looked down and he wasn't there.

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:16.260
And I said to Alexi, "I
don't know where Axel is."

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:18.720
And she said, "You're
holding him in your arms."

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:19.637
- [Kelly] Oh no.

00:06:19.637 --> 00:06:21.540
- And I was so tired that I looked down,

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:24.045
I was like, "Well, he's gone forever."

00:06:24.045 --> 00:06:25.440
(audience laughs)

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:27.030
- You know what, people don't get that.

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:30.510
You get a certain level of
tired that you literally...

00:06:30.510 --> 00:06:31.620
I guess normal people without kids,

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:33.000
it's when your sunglasses
are on your head.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:35.580
But it's more important
'cause it's a human.

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:36.450
But yeah...

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:38.460
- Alexi said the most
damning thing wasn't that

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:39.937
I didn't know he was
there, but when she said,

00:06:39.937 --> 00:06:41.700
"Oh, he's not lost forever,
you're holding him,"

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:43.694
my reaction was like, "Okay."

00:06:43.694 --> 00:06:44.527
(Kelly laughs)

00:06:44.527 --> 00:06:47.400
I wasn't even like, "Oh, thank goodness!"

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:48.660
- He's fine.

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:51.150
- I kind of liked the idea
that he was just lost.

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:52.260
- Oh, my god.

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:54.420
Seth did another podcast over the strike

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:56.400
with other late night hosts,
which I thought was brilliant.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:58.170
- Yeah, it was 12 episodes.

00:06:58.170 --> 00:06:59.917
We had a lot of free time on our hands.

00:06:59.917 --> 00:07:00.750
"Strike Force Five.

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:01.590
- But I thought it was really cool too

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:03.570
because a lot of people in this industry,

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:04.620
pits us against each other.

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:05.640
And I thought it was really cool too

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:06.900
that everybody was
together doing something.

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:07.814
- It was super nice.

00:07:07.814 --> 00:07:08.710
There've been different era of late night

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:12.060
where the hosts didn't get
along as well as we do now.

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:13.710
It was just for a good cause.

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:15.420
We were raising money
to help out our staffs

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:16.763
during the writer's strike.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:19.980
Obviously, we have a lot of similarities

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:21.240
but the longer we did the podcast,

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:23.460
I think we found our different lanes.

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:25.230
And it was a dream.

00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:26.400
It was really nice.

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:28.290
- I thought it was a
really cool idea as well.

00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:29.970
- Yeah, credit to Jimmy Kimmel

00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:31.050
who had the idea for the podcast.

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:32.610
- Yeah, I love him, yeah.

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:33.727
Let's do another break.

00:07:33.727 --> 00:07:35.790
"Late Night with Seth
Myers" airs weeknights

00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:39.300
at 12:35 AM on NBC and
streams on Peacock, everybody.

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:40.980
And don't miss Seth's podcast.

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:42.960
It's called "Family Trips
with the Myers Brothers."

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:44.760
It's available wherever
you get your podcasts.

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:46.653
We will be right back.

